\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qc\ul\b\f0\fs22\lang9 TRANSFORMER\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone\b0 The Transformer is the core deep learning architecture behind most modern Generative AI models, including GPT, ChatGPT, BERT, Gemini, Claude, Codex, and many image-generation models. Introduced by Google researchers in 2017 through the paper "Attention Is All You Need", the Transformer revolutionized AI by enabling models to understand relationships between words, sentences, and data more effectively than previous architectures such as RNNs and LSTMs. Its ability to process large amounts of data in parallel and capture long-range dependencies makes it the foundation of today's Generative AI systems.\par
\b\fs24 What is a Transformer?\par
\b0 A Transformer is a neural network architecture designed to process sequential data, such as text, by understanding the context and relationships between different elements in a sequence. Instead of processing information one word at a time, it analyzes all words simultaneously and determines which words are most relevant to each other.\par
For example, in the sentence:\par
"The animal didn't cross the road because it was tired."\par
The Transformer understands that the word "it" refers to "the animal", not the road. This contextual understanding is achieved through a mechanism called Attention.\par
\b Why Were Transformers Needed?\par
Before Transformers, AI models mainly used:\par
\par
\b0 1. Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs)\par

\pard{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf4\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Process words sequentially.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}Slow for large datasets.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}Struggle to remember information from earlier parts of long sentences.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 2. Long Short-Term Memory Networks (LSTMs)\par

\pard{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf4\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Improved memory compared to RNNs.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}Still process data one step at a time.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}Difficult to scale for very large models.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b Limitations\b0\par

\pard{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf4\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Slow training.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}Poor handling of very long contexts.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}Limited parallel processing.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}Transformers solved these issues by introducing self-attention and parallel computation.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b\fs28 Key Components of a Transformer\par
\fs24 1. Input Embeddings\b0\par
Computers cannot directly understand words. Therefore, words are converted into numerical vectors called embeddings.Key Components of a Transformer\par
\b 2. Positional Encoding\par
\b0 Since Transformers process all words simultaneously, they need information about word order.\par
Example:\par
"Dog bites man"\par
"Man bites dog"\par
Same words, different meanings.\par
Positional Encoding adds position information to each word embedding so the model understands sequence order.\par
\b 3. Self-Attention Mechanism\b0\par
This is the most important innovation of the Transformer.\par
Self-attention allows each word to determine which other words in the sentence are important for understanding its meaning.\par
Example\par
Sentence:\par
"The cat sat on the mat because it was comfortable."\par
When analyzing "it", the model pays more attention to "mat" than other words.\par
\ul\b Benefits\par

\pard{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf4\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone\b0 Understands context.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}Captures long-range relationships.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}Improves language understanding.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b 4. Query, Key and Value (Q, K, V)\par
\b0 To perform attention, each word generates three vectors:\par
Query (Q)\par
What information am I looking for?\par
Key (K)\par
What information do I contain?\par
Value (V)\par
What information should I pass on?\par
The model compares Queries and Keys to determine attention scores and then uses Values to build contextual understanding.\par
\b 5. Multi-Head Attention\b0\par
Instead of using a single attention mechanism, Transformers use multiple attention heads.\par
Each head focuses on different relationships:\par

